  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana or waited for the rule's first run. Its status was still \"pending\" when I created it.\n\n**Data:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` holds web access logs. The main fields are:\n- `timestamp`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `extension` and `url`\n- `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and `machine.os`\n- `response`, which is text, so I filtered on `response.keyword`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-431dd356 Web logs overview\" (id `29ed3b8f-2085-4eb1-ba91-65a485b35aae`) defaults to the last 7 days. It has five panels:\n- Total requests\n- 5xx responses\n- Average bytes\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n- Requests by response code (bar chart)\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-431dd356 5xx spike\" (id `603e4161-5321-4bf4-8354-50cbff0db606`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute. It matches `response.keyword` values from 500 to 599 and fires when the count over the last 5 minutes is above 10. It has no actions attached, so it records alerts in Kibana but doesn't notify anyone.",
